1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,050
Part 4

2
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:06,875
Hi everyone, in this session I'll be presenting my research about the social history

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:10,190
of Britain during the Industrial Revolution.

4
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:15,517
I particularly looked at how ordinary lives were affected by changes that happened at

5
00:00:15,517 --> 00:00:16,100
that time.

6
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,255
This was a time that saw the beginning of a new phenomenon:

7
00:00:20,255 --> 00:00:27,300
consumerism – where buying and selling goods became a major part of ordinary people's lives.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:32,039
In fact, it was in the 19th century that the quantity

9
00:00:32,039 --> 00:00:36,757
and quality of people's possessions was used as an indication of the wealth of

10
00:00:36,757 --> 00:00:37,480
the country.

11
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:42,483
Before this, the vast majority of people had very few possessions,

12
00:00:42,483 --> 00:00:46,300
but all that was changed by the Industrial Revolution.

13
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:51,493
This was the era from the mid-18th to the late 19th century,

14
00:00:51,493 --> 00:00:56,586
when improvements in how goods were made as well as in technology triggered massive

15
00:00:56,586 --> 00:01:02,220
social changes that transformed life for just about everybody in several key areas.

16
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:05,890
First let's look at manufacturing.

17
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:13,746
When it comes to manufacturing, we tend to think of the Industrial Revolution in images of steam engines

18
00:01:13,746 --> 00:01:14,630
and coal.

19
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:19,811
And it's true that the Industrial Revolution couldn't have taken place at all if

20
00:01:19,811 --> 00:01:22,080
it weren't for these new sources of power.

21
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:27,286
They marked an important shift away from the traditional watermills

22
00:01:27,286 --> 00:01:29,960
and windmills that had dominated before this.

23
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:35,620
The most advanced industry for much of the 19th century was textiles.

24
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:39,277
This meant that fashionable fabrics,

25
00:01:39,277 --> 00:01:42,230
and lace and ribbons were made available to everyone.

26
00:01:42,230 --> 00:01:50,376
Before the Industrial Revolution, most people made goods to sell in small workshops, often

27
00:01:50,376 --> 00:01:51,480
in their own homes.

28
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:57,477
But enormous new machines were now being created that could produce the goods faster

29
00:01:57,477 --> 00:01:59,403
and on a larger scale,

30
00:01:59,403 --> 00:02:01,890
and these required a lot more space.

31
00:02:01,890 --> 00:02:07,218
So large factories were built, replacing the workshops,

32
00:02:07,218 --> 00:02:09,410
and forcing workers to travel to work.

33
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:16,190
In fact, large numbers of people migrated from villages into towns as a result.

34
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:23,447
As well as manufacturing, there were new technologies in transport, contributing to the growth

35
00:02:23,447 --> 00:02:24,070
of consumerism.

36
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:29,204
The horse-drawn stagecoaches and carts of the 18th century,

37
00:02:29,204 --> 00:02:31,751
which carried very few people and goods,

38
00:02:31,751 --> 00:02:38,148
and travelled slowly along poorly surfaced roads, were gradually replaced by the numerous canals

39
00:02:38,148 --> 00:02:38,750
that were constructed.

40
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:43,690
These were particularly important for the transportation of goods.

41
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:52,112
The canals gradually fell out of use, though, as railways were developed, becoming the main way of moving goods

42
00:02:52,112 --> 00:02:55,200
and people from one end of the country to the other.

43
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:00,488
And the goods they moved weren't just coal, iron, clothes,

44
00:03:00,488 --> 00:03:03,713
and so on – significantly, they included newspapers,

45
00:03:03,713 --> 00:03:09,643
which meant that thousands of people were not only more knowledgeable about what was

46
00:03:09,643 --> 00:03:10,504
going on in the country,

47
00:03:10,504 --> 00:03:14,390
but could also read about what was available in the shops.

48
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:17,270
And that encouraged them to buy more.

49
00:03:17,270 --> 00:03:24,788
So faster forms of transport resulted in distribution becoming far more efficient – goods

50
00:03:24,788 --> 00:03:29,599
could now be sold all over the country, instead of just in the local

51
00:03:29,599 --> 00:03:29,860
market.

52
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:37,600
The third main area that saw changes that contributed to consumerism was retailing.

53
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:42,272
The number and quality of shops grew rapidly,

54
00:03:42,272 --> 00:03:48,128
and in particular, small shops suffered as customers flocked to the growing number of

55
00:03:48,128 --> 00:03:52,620
department stores – a form of retailing that was new in the 19th century.

56
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:59,276
The entrepreneurs who opened these found new ways to stock them with goods,

57
00:03:59,276 --> 00:04:01,441
and to attract customers:

58
00:04:01,441 --> 00:04:09,320
for instance, improved lighting inside greatly increased the visibility of the goods for sale.

59
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:16,584
Another development that made goods more visible from outside resulted from the use of

60
00:04:16,584 --> 00:04:17,788
plate glass,

61
00:04:17,788 --> 00:04:21,300
which made it possible for windows to be much larger than previously.

62
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:25,450
New ways of promoting goods were introduced, too.

63
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:33,388
Previously, the focus had been on informing potential customers about the availability of goods;

64
00:04:33,388 --> 00:04:39,040
now there was an explosion in advertising trying to persuade people to go shopping.

65
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:45,912
Flanders claims that one of the great effects of the Industrial Revolution was that

66
00:04:45,912 --> 00:04:47,720
it created choice.

67
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:48,195
All sorts of things that had previously been luxuries – from sugar to cutlery

68
00:04:48,195 --> 00:04:48,269
– became conveniences,

69
00:04:48,269 --> 00:04:48,525
and before long they'd turned into necessities:

70
00:04:48,525 --> 00:04:48,781
life without sugar or cutlery was unimaginable.

71
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:49,000
Rather like mobile phones these days!
